this is a story of vulnerable people  like matthew leahy who sought help for  mental health problems just explain what  happened to matthew well i'd like to  know to be honest  he entered  he was taken to a so-called place of  safety which was the linden center in  chelmsford  and within seven days my son was dead  matthew was found hanged in his room at  the center in essex his inquest raised  questions about his care indeed he  didn't have a care plan it was  fabricated by staff after his death  now essex mental health services is  subject to an inquiry and the initial  findings are that over a 21-year period  1500 people died either as in patients  or within three months of being  discharged  this is the early stages of the inquiry  we don't know yet what proportion of  those deaths were suicide how many could  have been prevented but talking to  families the inquiry chair says that she  already has seen examples of  unacceptable dispassionate care  however 70 families many who've  campaigned for years are refusing to  take part in the review process because  they want a full statutory public  inquiry with the power to call witnesses  they say the scale of the deaths  reinforces their argument  that's 1500 souls 1500 boys girls women  dementia patients it's 1500 people and  how many families destroyed originally  you were told weren't you that there  weren't enough people for a public  inquiry exactly um yeah i think nadine  doris words were we do not do public  inquiries into just  one death well here we go we've got 1500  now how many more have to die before the  statutory powers are given  but the inquiry chair who's heard from  14 families so far says people should  have confidence  my message to those people is please  come and talk to us  please come and talk to us your evidence  is really really valuable  your evidence and your ideas about how  we should be feeding this forward  improving the system you have unique  evidence we really want to hear it these  are his friends a couple of his friends  some such as the wade family have chosen  to give evidence their son richard also  died in the linden center  he was there for less than 12 hours  before the injuries that he inflicted  upon himself  by hanging himself in a bathroom where  someone had hanged themselves not three  months before  it boils down to something really quite  simple they didn't care  we don't want it to happen to others  because it's awful it changes everyone's  life  you know with friends us  family it just you know i'm not the same  as i was before  you know  there you go  essex partnership university trust say  they continue to support the ongoing  inquiry and encourage patients family  carers and staff to share their  experiences with the inquiry team jason  farrell sky news
 

